OUR TALKS were scarce compared to last year, and the crowds were fewer. 
But, on the plus side, there was a whole lot of new volunteers (thanks to 
the students, and KV Madhav of Engico and the lecturers of Dempo College, 
who motivated them to come).

In addition, there was a lot more of software shared, in the spirit of our 
LUG's goal -- freedom, software, sharing. We also made contacts with a number 
of potential GNU/Linux enthusiasts, and had an interesting 'open house' 
discussion in the evening attended by many students.

 	With so many computers being distributed in Goa
 	under the Cyberage scheme for college students, (according
 	to DigITal Goa's Niraj Naik, the latest number is now
 	close to 25,000), students obviously need to form a key
 	part of our growth plans. More so, since so many of our
 	excellent members have done well for themselves, and have
 	migrated elsewhere, or got involved in the serious world of work.

Besides the new volunteers and student faces around, there were also many an 
old-timer around. Thanks to DerekC and AshleyD for coming in early and doing 
the set-up. ArvindY and BijonS who were ever-ready to field all the many tech 
(and other!) questions. YunisS and Amit and Khandeparkar for entering just in 
time to add an impetus to the session.

Yunus had an introduction to his mobile-to-PC-via-SMS new interface, written in 
Python, and other stories to tell. MadhavKV came in later, inspite of having 
exams and class. Dr Anil Seth is someone we can always depend on. Young Randall 
put up an early appearance, and was there, as usual, to add a joke and a smile 
to the session.

On Sunday, September 5, we plan to introduce GNU/Linux to more people who might 
still be unaware of our activities (DP has made this point earlier). ILUG-Goa 
founder member Arvind Yadav will speak on "Linux In Business" at 4 pm, Sunday.

See you there! FN
