Debian Weekly News
http://www.debian.org/News/weekly/2004/36/
Debian Weekly News - September 14th, 2004
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Welcome to this year's 36th issue of DWN, the weekly newsletter for
the Debian community. Graham Williams has written a [1]book entitled
Debian GNU/Linux Desktop Survival Guide which is intended to deliver a
fun and productive environment. It guides you through the many
different regions of a GNU/Linux system with a focus on getting your
desktop environment to do what you want it to do.

  1. http://www.togaware.com/linux/survivor/

Preparation of Sarge CD and DVD Images. Steve McIntyre [2]explained
how dual layer DVD discs work and mentioned the types of CD and DVD he
would like to see supported for the upcoming sarge release. This
includes business card and network installation ISO images as well as
full 13 or 15 CD image sets for each architecture. He also mentioned
two 4.7 GB size DVD images and one 8.5 GB size double layer DVD image
for each architecture. The latter should only be provided via
[3]jigdo, though.

  2. http://lists.debian.org/debian-cd/2004/09/msg00042.html
  3. http://www.debian.org/CD/jigdo-cd/

Using Debian in commercial Environments. Somebody [4]asked on
Slashdot about experience shoe-horning Debian into a commercial
scenario when the third-party vendor (IBM) doesn't officially support
this distribution. He also mentioned about anecdotal evidence that
most of such software can be persuaded to work on Debian as well.

  4. http://ask.slashdot.org/askslashdot/04/09/07/2219255.shtml

GNOME 2.6 Transition complete. Jordi Mallach [5]reported that the
transition of GNOME 2.6 into sarge is finished. The last missing
package from the GNOME meta package, [6]Eye of GNOME has finally
entered testing. The only missing package is [7]gdm 2.6 which will
allow GNOME users to shutdown the computer directly when they close
their session.

  5. http://oskuro.net/blog/freesoftware/gnome-2.6-sarge-2004-09-08-01-39
  6. http://packages.debian.org/eog
  7. http://packages.debian.org/gdm

Debian.net Web Forums in Beta. Jeroen van Wolffelaar [8]announced
that since many people like web-based forums for discussion and
support, he would try providing a web forum for the Debian community.
The [9]web forums are still in beta, so any suggestions for
improvements or problem reports should be sent to
[10]admin@forums.debian.net. Of course, like all *.debian.net sites,
the forums are not an official Debian service, and it's been
[11]merged with [12]debianplaza.

  8. http://lists.debian.org/debian-project/2004/09/msg00014.html
  9. http://forums.debian.net/
  10. mailto:admin@forums.debian.net
  11. http://lists.debian.org/debian-project/2004/09/msg00024.html
  12. http://debianplaza.net/

Partman needs testing. Joey Hess [13]pointed out that recent changes
to partman have introduced a lot of bugs, including problems with
reiserfs and xfs. He doesn't know at this point if the new partman is
suitable to be in the next [14]debian-installer release. To help fix
the problem, he has called for volunteers to test partman in the daily
builds to not only do automated partitioning tests, but manual
partitioning to try all the strange combinations that are not often
used and make sure it works at least as well as it did in rc1.

  13. http://lists.debian.org/debian-boot/2004/09/msg00408.html
  14. http://www.debian.org/devel/debian-installer/

New legal Counsel for SPI. The Board of Directors of [15]Software in
the Public Interest, Inc. (SPI), Debian's umbrella organisation,
[16]announced the appointment of new legal counsel after the former
one, Chris Rourk, had resigned. The firm of [17]Cleary, Gottlieb,
Steen & Hamilton, represented by Greg Pomerantz, was willing to serve
as SPI's pro bono legal counsel and was duly appointed as such by the
board.

  15. http://www.spi-inc.org/
  16. http://lists.spi-inc.org/pipermail/spi-announce/2004/000100.html
  17. http://www.cgsh.com/

UserLinux releases first beta CD. Slashdot [18]discussed the [19]beta
release of [20]UserLinux, a new Debian-based distribution. Bruce
Perens explained that UserLinux is closely based on Debian and aims to
provide businesses with freely available, high quality GNU/Linux
operating systems accompanied by certifications, service, and support
options. Their i386 install CD is only 4.5 MB large, with the
remainder downloaded directly from Debian mirrors and they want to do
all of their Free Software development within Debian.

  18. http://linux.slashdot.org/article.pl?sid=04/09/10/1544203
  19. http://www.desktoplinux.com/news/NS2694988953.html
  20. http://www.userlinux.com/

Testing Debian-Installer Improvements. Joey Hess [21]reported about
the techniques he uses for [22]installer tests. The first and
preferred method includes creating a new netboot image to boot a
test-laptop. The second method refers to writing it on a USB stick to
boot from. He also [23]reported that he managed to pre-seed the
[24]debian-installer so that the first stage installer runs with only
one single keystroke.

  21. http://kitenet.net/~joey/blog/entry/my_debian-installer_test_methods-2004-09-12-00-24.html
  22. http://www.debian.org/devel/debian-installer/
  23. http://kitenet.net/~joey/blog/entry/one_keystroke_to_debian-2004-09-10-19-58.html
  24. http://www.debian.org/devel/debian-installer

Using Auto* Tools for Building Packages? Jesus Gonzalez-Barahona
[25]wondered if he should run [26]automake at build-time instead of
just using configure provided by upstream. Andrew Suffield
[27]asserted that this would render the package unbuildable once
automake changes. Marcelo Magallon [28]added that automake versions
are not compatible at source level and asked to keep the amount of
included cruft as small as possible.

  25. http://lists.debian.org/debian-devel/2004/09/msg00495.html
  26. http://packages.debian.org/automake
  27. http://lists.debian.org/debian-devel/2004/09/msg00500.html
  28. http://lists.debian.org/debian-devel/2004/09/msg00504.html

Patent Lawsuit Prohibition. Matthew Garrett had a [29]look at licenses
that are designed to discourage patent lawsuits. He noted that both
[30]Free Software Foundation and the [31]Open Source Initiative appear
to consider those licenses free, but the compatibility with the
[32]Debian Free Software Guidelines seems to be unclear. If Debian
decides to consider these (or some) licenses non-free, the project
needs to make it clear precisely why it holds this opinion, and then
set about trying to change other people's minds.

  29. http://people.debian.org/~terpstra/message/20040914.112541.76c4237d.en.html
  30. http://www.fsf.org/
  31. http://www.opensource.org/
  32. http://www.debian.org/social_contract#guidelines

System Binaries linked against unreachable Libraries. Fabrice Lorrain
[33]noticed that several programs in /sbin are linked against
libraries located in /usr/lib. These programs won't work unless /usr/
is mounted. Hence they should either be moved into /usr/sbin or the
libraries need to be moved into /lib instead. Jeroen van Wolffelaar
[34]explained that [35]lintian cannot warn about this currently.

  33. http://lists.debian.org/debian-qa/2004/09/msg00033.html
  34. http://lists.debian.org/debian-qa/2004/09/msg00035.html
  35. http://packages.debian.org/lintian

Security Updates. You know the drill. Please make sure that you update
your systems if you have any of these packages installed.

  * [36]webmin -- insecure temporary directory.

  36. http://www.debian.org/security/2004/dsa-544

